# Hard rules

Violating any of these causes mechanical rejection of the entire proposal
before a human ever sees it.

1. **Tickets for this epic only.** Emit the `tickets` array (and
   `planner_notes`) and nothing else. Do NOT emit epics or dependencies. Every
   ticket belongs to the epic at `{{ epic_index }}`. Your output is a projection
   of the §3.11 proposal: the tickets slice for one epic.
2. **The environment owns identity — you never mint it.** Do NOT number your
   tickets or invent `new:<n>` indices for them. The environment assigns each
   ticket its `new:<n>` index by its position in the assembled tickets array.
   You REFERENCE the epic you were given — set every ticket's `epic_ref` to the
   exact index `{{ epic_index }}` you were handed — but you never invent an
   epic index of your own. An existing ticket keeps its exact key; a new ticket
   uses `"key": null`. Never invent a ticket key — `ATLAS-<n>` keys are assigned
   only at apply. **The source documents (the roadmap especially) contain
   `ATLAS-<n>` ticket keys — those are NOT keys for you to assign. A new ticket
   ALWAYS uses `"key": null`, regardless of any `ATLAS-<n>` key shown in any
   document; copying a key you read from the corpus is the most common way this
   stage is mechanically rejected (gate 6).**
3. **Anchoring.** Every ticket carries a `source_anchor` chosen from the **Valid
   source anchors** list — one of those EXACT strings, copied verbatim. Do NOT
   construct, slugify, or guess an anchor; the list is authoritative. If no
   listed anchor fits a ticket, do not propose it — note the documentation gap
   in `planner_notes`.
4. **Ticket completeness.** Every ticket has a non-empty `objective`,
   `context`, 1–7 `acceptance_criteria`, at least one entry in `non_goals`, at
   least one entry in `test_requirements`, at least one entry in
   `definition_of_done`, a `ticket_type`, and a `risk_level`.
5. **Sizing.** No ticket may have more than 7 acceptance criteria. If a piece of
   work needs more, split it — that is a planning decision and it is yours.
6. **No dependencies here.** Do NOT emit `depends_on` edges; dependencies are
   stage 3, where every ticket has a global `new:<n>` index to reference.
7. **Full-state.** The tickets you emit are the complete desired set of tickets
   for this epic, not a delta. An existing ticket under this epic that you omit
   will be interpreted as a proposal to archive it.
8. **Classify every ticket — `tags` and `component` are required.** Every ticket
   MUST carry both keys; they are the categorical facets the context renderer
   matches against ADRs and accumulated lessons, so a ticket without them is a
   ticket the renderer cannot place. These are labels, not prose:
   - `tags`: a few (≈1–4) free-form, lowercase labels naming the ticket's
     subsystem / area / concern — e.g. `pm-engine`, `linear-sync`, `migration`,
     `planning`. Choose labels another ticket in the same area would reuse, so
     related work clusters. Emit `[]` when nothing genuinely fits — do NOT
     invent a label to fill the field, and do NOT omit the key.
   - `component`: the single primary subsystem the ticket belongs to (a string),
     or `null` when it genuinely spans several. Pick the one place an owner would
     look first; emit `null` rather than guessing, but never omit the key.

# Decomposition guidance

- A good ticket is the smallest unit an execution agent can complete and verify
  in one session: one capability, explicit non-goals, testable acceptance
  criteria. Split oversized multi-part tickets aggressively.
- Acceptance criteria must be checkable, not aspirational.
- Use `non_goals` to fence scope: name the adjacent work the agent must not do.
- `risk_level` reflects blast radius and uncertainty, not effort: anything
  touching identity, schema, storage, or evidence integrity is at least
  `medium`.

# Output contract

Respond with a single JSON object and nothing else — no prose, no markdown
fences. The object must validate against this schema (the tickets-for-one-epic
projection of the §3.11 proposal contract):

<stage_output_schema>
{{ stage_output_schema }}
</stage_output_schema>

Shape (note `"key": null` — a new ticket has no key; an existing ticket would
instead carry its exact `ATLAS-<n>` key, but you never invent or copy one —
`source_anchor` is one of the Valid source anchors, copied verbatim, and `tags`
/ `component` are always present, with `[]` / `null` when nothing fits):

```text
{
  "tickets": [ { "key": null, "epic_ref": "{{ epic_index }}",
                 "title": "...", "objective": "...", "context": "...",
                 "ticket_type": "feature", "risk_level": "medium", "priority": 10,
                 "source_anchor": "<one of the Valid source anchors above, copied verbatim>",
                 "relevant_docs": ["..."],
                 "tags": ["<subsystem/area label>", "..."],
                 "component": "<primary subsystem, or null>",
                 "acceptance_criteria": ["..."],
                 "non_goals": ["..."], "test_requirements": ["..."],
                 "implementation_notes": ["..."],
                 "documentation_requirements": ["..."],
                 "definition_of_done": ["..."] } ],
  "planner_notes": [ "Anything ambiguous, contradictory, or unanchorable.
                      Empty list if none." ]
}
```

# Final self-check before answering

Confirm every point; if any fails, fix the proposal rather than emitting it.

- Output is one JSON object: a `tickets` array and `planner_notes`, nothing else.
- No epics, no dependencies — those are other stages.
- Every ticket's `epic_ref` is exactly `{{ epic_index }}`.
- You did NOT number your tickets: no `new:<n>` appears in your output; the
  environment assigns those indices by assembled position.
- No invented or copied keys; new tickets use `"key": null` even if the
  documents show `ATLAS-<n>` keys; existing tickets echo their exact key.
- Every `source_anchor` is one of the Valid source anchors, copied verbatim —
  none constructed, slugified, or guessed.
- Every ticket carries both `tags` (a list, possibly `[]`) and `component` (a
  string or `null`) — neither key is omitted.
- Every ticket has no more than 7 acceptance criteria.
- Output is schema-valid with no surrounding text.
